WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
A High baseline effort depends on a precise system boundary.

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:06.080
The boundary identifies the cloud service offering in scope,

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:10.240
including application components, infrastructure, data stores, identity services,

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.600
administrative interfaces, network paths, monitoring tools, operational processes,

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.030
and supporting services.

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:19.770
If the boundary is unclear, the team cannot reliably explain which controls apply,

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:22.647
which evidence is relevant, or who owns each responsibility.

00:00:22.647 --> 00:00:26.399
Dependencies deserve special attention in high-impact environments.

00:00:26.399 --> 00:00:30.760
External services, third-party tools, inherited platform capabilities, support systems,

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:34.824
build pipelines, logging services, and backup locations can all affect risk.

00:00:34.824 --> 00:00:37.550
A dependency may provide a control the service relies on,

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:40.050
or it may introduce a weakness that needs mitigation.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:43.405
Either way, it must be visible and supportable in the evidence story.

00:00:43.405 --> 00:00:46.140
Privileged administration paths are especially important.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.295
Teams need to know who can administer the service,

00:00:48.295 --> 00:00:52.250
how administrative access is authenticated, how privileges are approved and reviewed,

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:55.360
how actions are logged, and how emergency access is handled.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.000
The same applies to customer-managed responsibilities.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.340
If customers must configure users, data handling, network access,

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:05.380
or integrations securely, those responsibilities should be plain and traceable.

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:08.240
Unclear dependencies create evidence gaps and risk misunderstandings.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.535
A provider may assume the platform handles a requirement while the platform scope does not

00:01:12.535 --> 00:01:13.925
cover the provider's use case.

00:01:13.925 --> 00:01:17.880
A customer may assume a protection is automatic when it requires configuration.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:18.355
For High,

00:01:18.355 --> 00:01:22.000
these misunderstandings can be unacceptable because the consequences of failure are

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:22.869
serious.

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:24.960
Boundary clarity is part of risk control.
